# AGENTS.md §1 — naming and semantics, machine-readable. This file is the single source of truth
# for the option values; AGENTS.md states the rules in prose and does not repeat the settings.
#
# The values below are adopted from the sibling repository Outpost.Warzone so that engine code
# moves between the two trees without a rename pass.
#
# This file owns IDENTIFIERS and semantics. /.clang-format owns whitespace, and the two never
# overlap: clang-tidy has no check that reports a wrong brace position or an over-long line, and
# clang-format cannot see that a parameter is missing its `_`.
#
# ── Status: CI gates this over GameLogic, on the Linux leg ────────────────────────────────
#
# REWRITTEN 2026-09-07 (slice 5d), BECAUSE EVERY WORD OF THE BLOCK THAT STOOD HERE WAS ABOUT A
# DIFFERENT REPOSITORY. This file was adopted whole from the sibling tree Outpost.Warzone so that
# engine code could move between the two without a rename pass, and the option values are still
# adopted -- but the STATUS, the projects and the reasons came with them. It claimed CI gated this
# over "GameLogic and NeuronServer", named `Build/CheckProjectFiles.py` and
# `Design/Archive/MmoScalabilityPlan.md`, and justified three exclusions by wire records, mesh
# vertices, GPU instance structs and "metres to eighth-metre lattice steps". None of those exists
# here. `NeuronServer` does not exist here. And NOTHING IN THIS REPOSITORY HAD EVER RUN
# CLANG-TIDY: `WarningsAsErrors: '*'` was a setting with no gate behind it, and the first sweep
# found thirty-six diagnostics in a tree this block called "swept clean".
#
# What is true now. `tools/check_tidy.py` sweeps `GameLogic/*.cpp` and CI runs it on the Linux leg
# of every push. It gets there through `Tests/PortableRunner/Shim`: `GameLogic/pch.h` includes
# `NeuronCore.h`, which includes `<WinSock2.h>`, so the real precompiled header cannot be parsed on
# Linux any more than `Outpost/` can be compiled there (Risk R15) -- and the portable runner had
# already built the stand-in. `Outpost/` is NOT swept: its DirectX and Win32 headers are the
# Windows job's, and a linter that has never seen them would bury this library's findings under
# theirs.
#
# Run it by hand on the files you wrote:
#
#   python tools/check_tidy.py GameLogic/YourNewFile.cpp
#
# Under LLVM 18 the library is clean as of slice 5d. Five diagnostics were code and were fixed --
# five locals in `ReadFlightControls` still wearing the `_` a parameter wears, and four dead stores
# that were transcriptions of a 6502 flag nothing reads, one of which was the fourth vestigial
# assignment this port has carried. The rest are excluded below, each with THIS repository's
# reason.
#
# Three rules of AGENTS.md §1 this file cannot express — R2's banned type affixes, R7's file naming
# and .vcxproj/.filters registration, and R11's standing spelling families — are checked by
# Build/CheckProjectFiles.py, which runs in the same CI job that builds. A check that exists
# somewhere is worth more than a rule everyone is asked to remember.
#
# readability-identifier-length is deliberately NOT enabled. Its defaults reject the domain
# vocabulary this codebase is built on — x, y, _a, _b — and short names here are precise, not lazy.

Checks: >
  readability-identifier-naming,
  bugprone-*,
  performance-*,
  modernize-*,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-branch-clone,
  -bugprone-implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result,
  -clang-analyzer-optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange,
  -modernize-use-trailing-return-type,
  -modernize-use-auto,
  -performance-enum-size

# THE EXCLUSIONS, EACH WITH THIS REPOSITORY'S REASON. Slice 5d measured every one of them against
# the tree rather than inheriting its justification, and three of the four it inherited turned out
# to fire nothing here at all -- they are enabled again and the sweep stays clean without them.
#
# `-bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters` stays, and it is the one exclusion the port genuinely
# needs. A routine that takes `(_a, _x, _y)` takes the 6502's three registers, in the order the
# original left them in; a routine that takes `(_high, _low)` takes a sixteen-bit value the way the
# machine held it. The check is right that they are swappable and wrong that this is a defect: the
# ORDER IS THE ORIGINAL'S and changing it to please a linter would break the correspondence every
# `// 6502:` comment in the tree depends on.
#
# `-bugprone-branch-clone` is off because this port transcribes 6502 CONTROL FLOW, and two branch
# instructions to the same label are two branches. `LL5`'s `CPX Q / BCC LL7 / BNE / CPY #64 /
# BCC LL7` is two tests and two branches to one destination; folding the two `if` bodies into one
# `||` would read better and would stop saying what the original does. The same shape is in the
# divider at the other site the check finds, where the comment on the first arm explains why the
# two paths reach the same statement for different reasons.
#
# `-bugprone-implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result` is off because the screen arithmetic is
# `int` on purpose. A cell offset is `cellRow * 8 * WIDTH`; the values are small, bounded by the
# canvas, and every one of the twenty-two sites is an index into a 64,000-byte array. Widening the
# operands to `std::size_t` would say that overflow was a live risk in a calculation whose largest
# term is 64,000.
#
# `-clang-analyzer-optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange` is off because it is wrong about scoped enums
# with a fixed underlying type. `ShipType` is `: std::uint8_t`, so C++ makes EVERY byte a value of
# the enumeration, and `TypeOf` exists precisely so that a sweep over all 256 can go through it --
# which several oracle suites do. The analyser reads the enumerator list and calls 224 out of
# range; the standard does not.
#
# NOTE ON THE FORMAT: `Checks:` is a FOLDED scalar, so a `#` line indented under it is not a
# comment — it folds into the value and corrupts the check list. Every comment belongs here.
#
# WarningsAsErrors: '*' says every enabled check is fatal, so that a future gate has one answer
# rather than two settings disagreeing about which findings matter.
WarningsAsErrors: '*'

# [/\\], not '/'. On a Windows Clang build the paths arrive with backslashes, so a filter anchored
# on a forward slash matches no header at all and silently suppresses every in-build diagnostic.
# Headers sitting DIRECTLY in a project folder, which is every hand-written one (AGENTS.md 3's
# flat-directory rule). The `.*` this replaced also matched build output -- the C++/WinRT
# projection under <Project>/x64/Debug/Generated Files/winrt/ -- and 46 of the 77 diagnostics in
# the first sweep were from headers no one in this repository wrote.
HeaderFilterRegex: '(NeuronCore|GameLogic|Outpost)[/\\][A-Za-z0-9]+\.h$'

CheckOptions:
  # ── Types: PascalCase, no prefix of any kind (R2, R5) ──────────────────────────────
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.NamespaceCase,            value: CamelCase  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.ClassCase,                value: CamelCase  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.StructCase,               value: CamelCase  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.EnumCase,                 value: CamelCase  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.EnumConstantCase,         value: CamelCase  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.TypeAliasCase,            value: CamelCase  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.TypedefCase,              value: CamelCase  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.ConceptCase,              value: CamelCase  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.TemplateParameterCase,    value: CamelCase  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.AbstractClassPrefix,      value: ''         }

  # ── Functions: PascalCase ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.FunctionCase,             value: CamelCase  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.MethodCase,               value: CamelCase  }

  # ── Constants: UPPER_CASE (R3); enumerators stay PascalCase above ──────────────────
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprVariableCase,    value: UPPER_CASE }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.GlobalConstantCase,       value: UPPER_CASE }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.ClassConstantCase,        value: UPPER_CASE }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.StaticConstantCase,       value: UPPER_CASE }

  # ── Storage prefixes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.PrivateMemberCase,        value: camelBack  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.PrivateMemberPrefix,      value: 'm_'       }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.ProtectedMemberCase,      value: camelBack  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.ProtectedMemberPrefix,    value: 'm_'       }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.ClassMemberCase,          value: camelBack  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.ClassMemberPrefix,        value: 'sm_'      }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.GlobalVariableCase,       value: camelBack  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.GlobalVariablePrefix,     value: 'g_'       }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.StaticVariableCase,       value: camelBack  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.StaticVariablePrefix,     value: 'g_'       }

  # R8: a public aggregate uses plain camelCase fields so brace initialization reads naturally.
  # m_ marks encapsulated state, not every field — wire records and Desc structs carry no prefix.
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.PublicMemberCase,         value: camelBack  }

  # ── Parameters carry a leading underscore (R1) ─────────────────────────────────────
  # Legal C++: the reserved forms are _Uppercase, anything containing __, and _lowercase at
  # GLOBAL scope. A parameter is never at global scope.
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.ParameterCase,            value: camelBack  }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.ParameterPrefix,          value: '_'        }
  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.LocalVariableCase,        value: camelBack  }

  - { key: readability-identifier-naming.MacroDefinitionCase,      value: UPPER_CASE }
